F = {
 "species|Aarakocra": "Birdfolk who nest in tall mountain trees, many of them settlers from the Elemental Plane of Air. They serve as scouts and explorers in a strange world. Feathered and taloned, they feel most at home in the sky and fly with grace and speed.",
 "species|Aasimar": "Mortals who carry a spark of the Upper Planes in their souls. They can arise among any people and often show a halo, glowing eyes, or metallic freckles. They use that spark to shed light, heal wounds, and strike with heavenly anger.",
 "species|Aetherborn": "Aetherborn live only a few years or even months, so they treat every moment as precious. They chase pleasure and sensation, each in their own way, and try to wring every bit of joy from their short lives.",
 "species|Astral Elf": "Elves who left the Feywild long ago to live closer to their gods on the Astral Plane. Starlight shines in their eyes. Nothing ages there, so many are very old, and some grow melancholy while others seem to feel little.",
 "species|Autognome": "Mechanical beings built by rock gnomes. Some break away from their makers through a malfunction or odd circumstance and set out alone. They look like their creators, speak Gnomish, and run on parts as different as clockwork, stardust, or even a beating heart.",
 "species|Aven": "Humanoids of Naktamun with the wings and heads of birds. Some have hawk heads and short wings for fast flight, others have ibis heads and broad wings for soaring. They love to fly over their foes and use speed to outwit them.",
 "species|Boggart": "Small, squat goblinoids of Lorwyn-Shadowmoor with horns and animal snouts. Each looks quite different from the next. They love brewing potions and tend to specialize. In Lorwyn they are born into noisy warrens where rules are only suggestions.",
 "species|Bugbear": "Hulking, long limbed cousins of goblins and hobgoblins, with coarse hair and pointed teeth. Their roots lie in the Feywild, and they kept a fey knack for lurking just out of sight. Despite their size, they are quiet skulkers, and many have slipped away from Maglubiyet.",
 "species|Centaur": "Ravnican centaurs love open space and the freedom to roam, even in a crowded city. They run through plazas, parks, and ruins, hooves pounding, until a wall stops them. They have a muscular human upper body on a horse's frame.",
 "species|Changeling": "Shapeshifters who can take on any face they like, so they live unnoticed in many societies. Their craft comes from the Feywild, where the first changelings appeared. In true form they look faded and plain, and some use new faces to reveal part of their souls.",
 "species|Deep Gnome": "Also called svirfneblin, these gnomes are natives of the Underdark and carry its magic. They can blend into stone and are hard to find by magic. These gifts let them survive for generations in danger, and they can live up to 500 years.",
 "species|Dhampir": "Living people with vampiric strength who suffer a dark hunger, usually for blood but sometimes for dreams or life energy. Many come from a vampire's descendants, a bite that changed them only in part, or a grim bargain. Each must decide whether to master the hunger or yield to it.",
 "species|Dragonborn": "Descendants of eggs laid by chromatic and metallic dragons, possibly blessed by Bahamut and Tiamat, or perhaps made by dragons alone. They look like wingless, scaly, horned dragons on two legs. Their color and features echo the dragon they descend from.",
 "species|Dragonborn (Chromatic)": "Dragonborn who inherit the raw power of black, blue, green, red, and white dragons. Those colors shine in their scales and in the energy of their breath. They embody volcanoes, arctic winds, and lightning storms, and also the quiet menace of swamp and forest.",
 "species|Dragonborn (Gem)": "Dragonborn descended from gem dragons, who call themselves heirs of Sardior, the Ruby Dragon. Amethyst, crystal, emerald, sapphire, and topaz hues glow in their scales. Their gifts are the power of mind and will, bright insight, and the joy of discovery, but also the dry ache of despair.",
 "species|Dragonborn (Metallic)": "Dragonborn who claim the tenacity of brass, bronze, copper, gold, and silver dragons, whose colors glint in their scales. They stand for the fire of hearth and forge, cold mountain air, a spark of inspiration, and acid that scours things clean.",
 "species|Duergar": "Dwarves changed by centuries in the deepest Underdark, which soaked them in strange magic. Mind flayers and other aberrations later experimented on them and left them with psionic powers. They eventually broke free of those masters and built their own lives below and beyond.",
 "species|Dwarf": "Dwarves live in old kingdoms and halls carved into mountain roots, among ringing picks and roaring forges. They stay loyal to clan and tradition and hate goblins and orcs. Short, broad, and hardy, they are skilled warriors, miners, and workers of stone and metal.",
 "species|Dwarf (Kaladesh)": "Kaladesh dwarves judge beauty by quality, so the best device is one that works for years with little upkeep. They despise laziness and shortcuts. A good artisan, they say, works steadily and with care, and they take great pride in that work ethic.",
 "species|Eladrin": "Elves of the Feywild who can step from place to place in a blink. Each resonates with a season that sets their mood and look, and the season can shift, though some stay in one forever. Like other elves, they live over 750 years.",
 "species|Elf": "A magical people of otherworldly grace who live in ancient forests or silvery spires lit by faerie light. They love nature, magic, art, music, and poetry. Slender and fine featured, they seem hauntingly beautiful to many other folk.",
 "species|Elf (Kaladesh)": "Kaladesh elves are tuned to the flow of aether, which they call the Great Conduit. They believe the only way to truly grasp it is to join it, shaping the flow while it shapes them. This sensitivity gives them a deep bond with all life.",
 "species|Elf (Zendikar)": "Fearless, adaptable people who have fared better than most in Zendikar's chaos. They meet the Eldrazi with resilience and courage. Their treetop villages regrow almost as fast as they fall, and their shamans and druids channel green mana, the magic of life and growth.",
 "species|Elves of Many Realms": "Corellon Larethian made the first elves as fey beings who changed shape at will. After they were exiled for plotting with Lolth, they fled to the Feywild. There they became Humanoids and lost that gift, and they often wept over the loss.",
 "species|Faerie": "Playful, mischievous beings with insect bodies, antennae, black eyes, and wings. Every faerie is born from a flower and has innate magic, which many use for pranks. In Lorwyn some serve Queen Oura, while in Shadowmoor others may follow Queen Maralen.",
 "species|Fairy": "Small winged folk of the Feywild who look like tiny elves with insect wings. Each has one special physical trait. The first fairies spoke Elvish, Goblin, or Sylvan, and meeting human visitors led many to learn Common too.",
 "species|Firbolg": "Distant kin of giants who once wandered primeval forests, where the forest magic wove itself into their souls. That magic still hums inside them, even far from any forest. It lets them move unseen and speak with plants and animals, and they live up to 500 years.",
 "species|Flamekin": "People made of fire and stone, with bodies that give off harmless magical flames and a strong tie to nature. They hold self-discovery and self-expression as life's noblest goal, a lifelong road they call the Path of Flame. They live in Lorwyn or Shadowmoor.",
 "species|Genasi": "Genasi are children of genies and mortals who carry the power of the elemental planes in their blood. Those planes are harsh for visitors, with crushing earth, searing flame, endless sky, and boundless sea. Genies move through the mortal world with ease.",
 "species|Giff": "Tall, broad shouldered folk with hippo-like features, impossible to miss wherever they go. Their home world has faded into legend, and the gods who made them are forgotten. Those gods' petrified bodies drift on the Astral Sea, unrecognized.",
 "species|Gith": "The gith rose up against the mind flayers who enslaved them, led by the hero they are named for. After winning freedom, two factions disagreed about what kind of civilization to build. The quarrel became open hatred, and they remain bitter enemies who fight to the death on sight.",
 "species|Githyanki": "Once free from mind flayer slavery, the githyanki split from the githzerai and fled to the Astral Plane. There they sharpened their psionic power and built the city of Tu'narath. They now spread across the multiverse from outposts called creches. Lanky and strong, they have yellow, green, or brown skin.",
 "species|Githzerai": "After the schism with the githyanki, githzerai moved to Limbo, a swirling storm of matter and energy. Their strong psionic will carved out a home within the chaos. Slender and speckled in yellow, green, or brown, some now explore other planes and worlds.",
 "species|Gnome": "Magical folk shaped by gods of invention, illusion, and life underground, including Garl Glittergold. Early gnomes hid in forests and burrows and beat predators with traps and mazelike tunnels. Petite, big eyed, and pointed eared, they make up in cleverness what they lack in size.",
 "species|Goblin": "Most Ravnicans see goblins as wretched and unimportant, but goblins expect to be recognized someday. They survive among bigger, stronger creatures through tenacity, agility, cunning, and luck. Small and wiry, about three feet tall, they have warty green or red skin.",
 "species|Goblin (Dankwood)": "Goblins of the Dankwood forest, which may once have held a Netherese wizard's sanctum. They are gentler and more artistic than most goblins. They are curious about everything and share a magical bond with the small creatures of the woods.",
 "species|Goblin (Ixalan)": "Native to Ixalan, these goblins are curious and eager, and they joined the Brazen Coalition pirates on first contact. Some still live in remote jungle colonies. About three feet tall, they crouch and scramble with prehensile tails, and make fine lookouts and rope hands on pirate ships.",
 "species|Goliath": "Towering distant descendants of giants, each carrying the favors of the first giants as supernatural boons. Some resemble stone giants, others fire giants. They can briefly grow toward giant size, and they follow their own path, free of the old feuds among giantkind.",
 "species|Hadozee": "Descended from tiny treetop mammals that evolved gliding flaps, hadozees are now sapient and eager to explore beyond their dangerous homeworld. Natural climbers, they have dexterous feet and skin membranes between arms and legs that stretch taut to glide.",
 "species|Halfling": "Halflings want a quiet home, a warm fire, a good meal, and good company, far from monsters and war. Some farm in remote villages, while others travel in nomadic bands, drawn by the open road. Even wanderers love peace, food, and hearth.",
 "species|Harengon": "Rabbitfolk from the Feywild who embody freedom and travel and carry its exuberance into other worlds. They have long feet, colorful fur, sharp senses, and strong legs, and they brim with energy. A little fey luck often lands them just clear of danger.",
 "species|Hexblood": "People infused with fey magic through eldritch energy or odd witchcraft, often after a deal with a powerful hag. Their strange power shows up as eerie clairvoyance and beguiling magic, and it marks them as people touched by the fey.",
 "species|Hobgoblin": "Born in the ancient courts of the Feywild alongside goblins and bugbears, many were driven out and marshaled as soldiers by Maglubiyet. They still follow the Feywild rule of reciprocity, where a gift bonds giver and receiver. This helps them build tight communities and vast legions.",
 "species|Human": "The youngest of the common peoples, humans live short lives and push to achieve much in their years. Perhaps they feel they must prove themselves to the elder races. They build mighty empires through conquest and trade, and they are innovators, achievers, and pioneers.",
 "species|Human (Amonkhet)": "The most diverse and adaptable of Amonkhet's five humanoid peoples. Humans train in many fighting styles, use any color of mana, and serve all five gods. No one facing a human can predict the fight, which may show cunning or ruthlessness.",
 "species|Human (Innistrad)": "Most of Innistrad's people are human, and player characters usually should be too. Still, the folk of Kessig, Gavony, Nephalia, and Stensia differ a great deal. Variety among characters comes from their home province instead of their species.",
 "species|Human (Ixalan)": "Humans live across Ixalan, in Torrezon, now held by the Legion of Dusk, and in the Sun Empire. The Legion's vampires were once human, and the peasants they rule still are. Some who fled the Legion formed the Free Cities and later the Brazen Coalition.",
 "species|Human (Kaladesh)": "Kaladesh humans are passionate and curious. At their best they chase grand, nearly impossible goals. At their worst they are shallow, impractical dreamers who ignore consequences. Their ambition has shaped the plane's history and still guides its fate.",
 "species|Human (Keldon)": "The people of Keld are human but unlike any others. Adults stand over six feet tall, with heavy muscle and gray skin from ashy white to blue gray. They shrug off cold and prize self-reliance, strength, and courage above all.",
 "species|Human (Zendikar)": "The most numerous, diverse, and adaptable folk of Zendikar, and the core of most exploring expeditions. Many hide behind walls in tiny villages and outposts, but many go out to explore and defend the world. As the Eldrazi spread, more are driven from safety.",
 "species|Kalashtar": "Kalashtar come from the union of humans with renegade quori spirits from the plane of dreams. They look human with symmetrical, slightly angular features, and their eyes glow with strong emotion. They sense their spirit as instinct and inspiration, which grants minor psionic power.",
 "species|Kender": "Small humanoids who look like pointy eared humans, born when Reorx's wild creation changed some gnomes. They gained near supernatural curiosity and fearlessness. That inquisitiveness drives them to adventure, and many tumble through portals into new worlds.",
 "species|Kenku": "Raven-like feathered folk with sharp observation and perfect memories. No kenku recalls their origin, and they joke that there are as many tales as kenku. Some call it a curse of flightless mimicry, others a blessed mission to watch and catalog wonders.",
 "species|Khenra": "Tall, lean folk of Amonkhet with jackal heads and sleek dark fur. They think biting is an unworthy way to fight. Nearly all are born twins, and each pair shares a close emotional bond, so one twin's death in the trials is a terrible shock.",
 "species|Khoravar": "Descendants of humans and elves who built their own communities in Khorvaire, and who dislike the term half-elf. Their name means children of Khorvaire in Elvish. Many see themselves as a bridge between cultures and become bards, diplomats, mediators, or translators.",
 "species|Kithkin": "Short folk with stout legs, long arms, and broad, expressive faces. Most are joined by an empathic web that lets them feel nearby kithkin, so they trust each other deeply and see betraying their own as a heinous crime. Some leave the web, often after Phyrexian trauma.",
 "species|Kobold": "Among the smallest draconic creatures, kobolds show their ancestry in glinting scales and roars. Legends say the first came from the Underdark near early dragons. Some serve or worship dragons, while others help defend against them. Their scales are usually rust colored.",
 "species|Kor": "Nomadic, reverent people devoted to the land and its sacred sites, living spare lives on the move. With ropes and hooks they climb cliffs and cross chasms so deftly they seem to fly. Tied to white mana, they value harmony, order, and strong tradition.",
 "species|Leonin": "Lion-like nomads who guard Oreskos, a golden plain that even gods rarely enter. Prides keep to themselves, distrusting strangers. Tall and tawny furred, some with manes from gold to black, a few wonder what lies past the border mountains and go out to test themselves.",
 "species|Lizardfolk": "Scaled people some sages call distant cousins of dragonborn and kobolds, though they are their own folk and have lived on the Material Plane since its creation. The gods gave them tough bodies and a link to nature, so they can survive on wits alone. Myths say they guard natural wonders.",
 "species|Locathah": "Proud, resilient fish-folk who have endured war, slavery, and mistreatment at the hands of other aquatic creatures. They live in tribal communities beneath the water along seacoasts. They hunt both above and below the waves.",
 "species|Lorwyn Changeling": "Charismatic shapeshifters who crudely mimic creatures and plants, but only on the surface. They always keep blue green skin, tentacle-like fur tufts, and bulbous yellow eyes, so a changeling tree or elk is easy to spot. Many Lorwyn folk find the plain disguises charming.",
 "species|Loxodon": "Elephant headed humanoids over seven feet tall, with trunks, tusks, and thick leathery skin. They are calm presences in Ravnica's busy streets, humming or sitting very still. If provoked, they bellow and trumpet with rage. They are wise, loyal, and unshakable guild members.",
 "species|Lupin": "Bristling, snarling folk who cast imposing shadows in the Mists. They often arise when a werewolf's curse only partly takes hold, leaving them stuck in a wolflike hybrid form. This gives them bestial might, sharp instincts, and a dread-inducing howl.",
 "species|Merfolk": "Zendikar's merfolk are curious, thoughtful, analytical scholars and explorers. They once organized around three deities, then learned their faith rested on a corrupted memory of the Eldrazi titans. Facing the Eldrazi, they set aside old quarrels and united.",
 "species|Merfolk (Ixalan)": "The River Heralds are amphibious humanoids at home in Ixalan's oceans, rivers, and rain forests. They stand seven to eight feet tall, with skin from burgundy and violet to green and orange. Fins run along their arms, backs, and calves, and they breathe air or water.",
 "species|Minotaur": "Strong, dedicated, and brave, Ravnican minotaurs are at home on the battlefield. They fight with burning fury yet keen tactics, making them fine commanders and shock troops. Barrel chested with bull heads, they often decorate or sheathe their horns in metal.",
 "species|Minotaur (Amonkhet)": "Powerfully built, barrel chested humanoids with ram heads and horns curled around their ears. They are strong, stubborn, boastful, and recklessly brave, and they relish a fight against long odds. Rowdy and blunt to the point of rudeness, they say what they want and dare others to stop them.",
 "species|Monstrous Adventurers": "Some campaigns let normally sinister humanoids join the party as heroes. This option is for DMs who want to widen their selection of species. Playing a creature usually cast as a villain offers comedy, tragedy, or antihero stories, so talk it over first.",
 "species|Naga": "Giant snake bodies with a humanoid torso, arms, and shoulders, adorned with armor and jewelry. They hold their upper bodies off the ground and can lower them to move faster. Naga follow the Sweetest Harmony, a perfect balance between body and mind.",
 "species|Orc": "Gruumsh made orcs and gave them gifts for roaming plains, caverns, and seas and facing monsters there. They keep endurance, determination, and darkvision even when they follow other gods. Tall, broad, and gray skinned with tusklike canines, they hear tales of their ancestors' long journeys.",
 "species|Orc (Ixalan)": "Orcs came from the mountains where Torrezon was born and have fought the Legion of Dusk since it began. The Church of Torrezon declared them anathema, and only a few hundred remain. Many serve on Brazen Coalition ships, doing work that needs plenty of muscle.",
 "species|Owlin": "Distant kin of giant owls from the Feywild, in sizes from small and fluffy to wide winged and majestic. They have arms and legs like other Humanoids, plus wings, and their feathers make no sound. Some are nocturnal, others simply night owls who rise late.",
 "species|Plasmoid": "Shapeless beings who take on a similar shape to whoever is nearby, yet are never mistaken for anything else. They eat by osmosis like an amoeba and breathe through pores. Usually translucent gray, they can change color by absorbing dyes, and their flexible limbs handle tools and weapons.",
}
